        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"center\":5,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":4,\"age_fit\":5,\"continuity\":4,\"delight\":4},\"evidence\":{\"center\":\"Lennon acts in nearly every level. He volleys the cuju ball through the shrinking hole, dribbles past 'three farmers and a very confused goose,' runs the vote in the tavern, takes the first penalty against McCrum, grabs the flickering trophy, unwraps the trophy Pickles found, and scores the final penalty. His stated likes (soccer, penalties, his shootout game) drive the premise and the climax. Level 6 is the thin spot: he says 'Not on my watch' and then only watches, though that line and his 'Fifty YEARS?' in Level 8 still count as reactions of his own.\",\"craft\":\"The shape is strong: a cold open in the backyard at the fence, the glowing ball as the launch device, a deletion beat in most levels, and a return home with the pixel-message keepsake. Level 6 has no deletion or challenge, and its coin arrives for nothing. The Level 8 montage, headed '1958 to 2022,' jumps back to the 1920s mid-reel. The closing line is duplicated verbatim after 'The End.'\",\"facts\":\"Most facts are accurate and woven into action: the 1930 two-ball final, Gaetjens and the part-time players, Pickles, Ken Aston's traffic-light cards, the Dick, Kerr Ladies ban, and Chastain. Three weaknesses stand out. 'Newspapers back in England thought there must have been a typo!' is a popular legend stated without a hedge. 'The very first penalty spot in history' is anachronistic, because in 1891 the kick was taken from a 12-yard line and the spot came in 1902. The net with a single hole belongs to later, Song-era cuju, not to 'more than two thousand years ago.'\",\"age_fit\":\"The count of 2633 is within the 2200-3200 range. Peril is moderate and resolves within each level. The humour suits a 10-year-old ('the most sensible silly idea I've ever heard,' 'Okay, that one doesn't count,' he told nobody). Vocabulary and sentence length fit band C.\",\"continuity\":\"The story reuses Bit, 'PLAYER ONE,' BZZZT, 'Level complete,' the watery pixel eyes, and coins fusing into a trophy, and it recalls the video-game story. It does not echo the previous ending ('THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD') in the opening. It also drops the bible rule that Bit jokes about being 8-bit; he never does.\",\"delight\":\"It reads aloud well, with strong sound words (Thud, CLANG, ding) and jokes that work for both parent and child (the grumpy '...aye,' the goose, 'The best boy'). Two things let it down: the repeated final line after 'The End,' which reads like a glitch, and the hollow 'Lennon cracked his knuckles. \\\"Not on my watch.\\\"' that leads to nothing.\"},\"caps_applied\":[\"none\"],\"disqualified\":false,\"overall\":4.35,\"editor_notes\":[\"Give Lennon a real beat in Level 6, where he currently watches after promising action. For example, have the scoreboard flicker and let him hold the 1-0 in place, or have him spot the Gaetjens cross coming. Also remove the duplicated 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' after 'The End.'\",\"Tighten the history. Hedge the England 'typo' newspaper story as a legend. Change 'the very first penalty spot' to a twelve-yard line, noting that the spot came later. Say the hole-in-the-net cuju goal came in later centuries. Put the Dick, Kerr Ladies flash in date order or fix the Level 8 heading.\",\"Honour the bible more fully. Have the opening echo last time's 'THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD,' for example with Lennon mid-build on his shootout game. Give Bit at least one joke about being 8-bit, such as the feathery cuju ball rendering in higher resolution than he does.\"],\"best_moment\":\"\\\"No kicking people in the shins?\\\" A long pause. Then, grumpily: \\\"...aye.\\\"\",\"worst_moment\":\"Lennon cracked his knuckles. \\\"Not on my watch.\\\"\"}"
